is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — "not found" for all jeremysitlab.com names — is not supported by any live evidence. A complete four-phase sweep found the campus DNS infrastructure operating correctly in every measurable dimension.

**Affected component:** None. srv1 (10.5.0.4) runs BIND9 (`named`, pid=29) configured as an authoritative-only server for `jeremysitlab.com`. It has no recursion (`recursion no; allow-query { any; }`), the zone file contains correct A records for `srv1`, the apex domain, and a wildcard covering all other names under `jeremysitlab.com` — all pointing to 10.5.0.4. No nftables or iptables rule filters port 53. The tc qdisc on eth0 is the Linux default (`fq_codel`), with no netem delay or token-bucket shaping.

**Mechanism:** Live `nslookup srv1.jeremysitlab.com` and `www.jeremysitlab.com` from both pc1 (10.1.0.0/24) and pc3 (10.3.0.0/24) return `10.5.0.4` in under 1 ms. Direct `dig @10.5.0.4` also confirms NOERROR answers. srv1 is IP-reachable at 0% loss. The automated `service_snapshot` flagged a failure only because it tested the bare hostname `srv1` without a search domain — a bare name queried against an authoritative-only (no-recursion) server is legitimately REFUSED, not a DNS server fault.

**Root cause:** There is no fault. The DNS server is healthy and correctly serving the jeremysitlab.com zone. The user's "not found" experience cannot be reproduced against any FQDN under jeremysitlab.com.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required on srv1. If users expect bare-hostname resolution (`srv1` instead of `srv1.jeremysitlab.com`), adding `search jeremysitlab.com` to client `/etc/resolv.conf` would resolve those queries via the wildcard A record already in the zone.
=== END ===